ry  dangerous disease that can kill and  is killing many people in those areas  they are taking risks because  uh unlike the people who will use this  vaccine  after it's been proved safe in these  large populations um  this is still experimental they are  still checking on the safety they're  still  checking on the efficacy but um  it's a it's a gamble if you will that is  reasonable to take  and therefore a very fair offer i think  to the drop at this point  we mentioned one corona vaccine being  very successful one less successful  there  are hundreds in development right now  but what is the most ethical way of  testing a more promising one and a less  promising one  you know we don't know that which  vaccine is successful at this time  it's it's a bit like uh calling the  elections uh after half an hour  of results pouring in things still will  change um  but um still when there are signals that  one vaccine might be  more promising than another it may be  tempting to say  oh it's not okay to compare them anymore  to each other because we're offering  the people who are getting that at that  point less promising vaccine  a less promising alternative but  remember it's really like the elections  um  it is very nice just briefly  why is it important to test vaccines on  on the continent or country in which  they're to be used  well um it's actually very important for  that country  and its populations not only is it fair  towards uh  the participants and their families who  get potentially protected by the vaccine  if it works  it's also important for the inhabitants  of that country to know  this vaccine was tested in people like  us who have our  nutritional and environmental and maybe  even genetic um  characteristics um and it will also  help that country i think in the  negotiations later about which country  will be getting the vaccine if it's  proven  efficacious so it's actually very  valuable for a country  to be a test site for vaccine trials  okay neil stay where you are we'll  return to you later in the show  first of all biontech and pfizer have  sparked a surge of  optimism but the unsung heroes of this  tale  are the thousands of unpaid volunteers  taking part in clinical trials  many in latin america however it's not  clear when  latin americans will actually get the  vaccine  in sao paulo biontech researchers have  already tested the vaccine on hundreds  of people  either with an active ingredient or  placebo latin america is one of the  world's main vaccine testing grounds  largely in brazil volunteers are not  allowed to talk to the media  they're unpaid for many of the  attraction is that they will at least be  vaccinated by taking part  our test subjects are not just health  workers they're also ordinary brazilians  who have to get up early to take the bus  or subway for hours to get to work  brazil is the country with the second  most coronavirus related deaths in the  world  do the people here feel like guinea pigs  in a  of course there's a lab that's  unavoidable when testing the vaccine  monday's announcement showed that  biontech's research was more advanced  than any of its competitors vaccines  the german company stressed the test  results were early and still incomplete  will be efficient it will probably be  one of the most important vaccines in  the world  another german pharmaceutical company  curavac is also testing a vaccine in  latin america  in panama the high infection rate among  the population makes it easier to get  speedy results  the panamanians are hopeful that the  cooperation will allow them to  manufacture the vaccine itself later  technology transfer in return for  helping with the testing phase  the fact that panama is working together  with the german vaccine company  certainly gives our country a  competitive advantage over other  countries  kurovak is also testing in peru  for the inhabitants of the poor area  bella vista del mirador  the testing process itself is not the 