se we still don't have surefire  therapies to treat someone  if an induced covet 19 infection turns  severe  the possibility that a human challenge  trial could cause a volunteer  long-term damage or even kill them that  can't be ruled out  despite those risks britain is  apparently not the only country to  consider conducting human challenge  trials  according to the science journal nature  at least some  exploratory planning has commenced in  belgium  and the us as well  and returning to our professor at  bioethics  what's your take on human challenge  trials  i think that child trials quite distinct  from what we've discussed so far which  are regular  field trials challenge trials are  also in my view ethical that's a less  consensus view than  the one that i've expressed earlier  challenge trials  would be acceptable in my own view  because the risks although they exist  remain  lower for mortality than a practice that  everybody accepts such as  live kidney donation only in this case  um we're doing it not just to help one  person but of course with  the informed consent of the person being  put at risk but to help  hundreds and thousands and millions of  people  uh if we can get to a vaccine faster i  think  it's unbalanced justified with the  informed consent of the volunteers  in what ways can they help speed up the  development of a vaccine  in multiple ways in a field trial you're  waiting for people to get  exposed to the virus and that sometimes  doesn't happen  it has not happened in some of the early  trials for  a vaccine to fight coronavirus in the uk  it hasn't happened in the trials of  other types  for coronavirus countermeasures in china  so  if there wouldn't be enough exposure to  the virus  in a regular trial we wouldn't know for  many many months  whether the vaccine protects against the  virus or not in a child trial you simply  deliberately infect people  the flip side of this is that uh you  know that it works both as far as  and you can 